menuconfig HCP_MODBUS
	bool "HCP Modbus RTU master"
	default y
	depends on SERIAL
	select MODBUS if HCP_MODBUS_BACKEND_SUBSYS
	select GPIO
	help
	  C++ facade over an RTU client, on the UART the board's `zephyr,modbus-serial`
	  node names - which also carries the RS485 DE pin. No third-party Modbus
	  implementation is involved either way.

	  MODBUS is only selected when the subsys backend is chosen on purpose: its
	  modbus_serial driver instantiates for the same devicetree node and would claim
	  the UART and the DE pin at init even when nothing calls it.

if HCP_MODBUS

choice HCP_MODBUS_BACKEND
	prompt "RTU framing backend"
	default HCP_MODBUS_BACKEND_NATIVE

config HCP_MODBUS_BACKEND_SUBSYS
	bool "Zephyr's in-tree subsys/modbus"
	help
	  The subsys owns RTU framing, t3.5 inter-frame timing and the DE line.

	  Not the default, and it is not a preference: on the bench meter it refused
	  every reply although a raw read of the same wire showed a CRC-perfect frame each
	  time (see rtu.h for the measurements), and it logs nothing about the bytes it
	  rejected. It stays selectable because no board but the C3 has been run with
	  hardware attached, so if the subsys turns out to work for another slave, that is
	  a measured fact to compare against rather than an assumption.

	  If you choose it, prj.conf must also assign CONFIG_MODBUS_ROLE_CLIENT=y (a
	  choice symbol cannot be selected from another symbol); CMake stops the build
	  with that instruction if it is missing.

config HCP_MODBUS_BACKEND_NATIVE
	bool "hcp's own RTU frame engine over hcp::serial::Port"
	depends on HCP_SERIAL
	help
	  Frames a reply by the length its REQUEST implies, under one overall deadline,
	  and prints the bytes it rejected. Ported behaviour from the previous firmware's
	  master (esp-mb's portserial.c: flush the receiver, then read a length that is
	  known rather than guessed from line idle).

	  The default on every board. See include/hcp/modbus/rtu.h for the measurements
	  that decided it; the C3 is the board where that was proven with a real slave.

endchoice

comment "The role choice (MODBUS_ROLE_CLIENT) is set in prj.conf: Kconfig"
comment "choices cannot be selected from a symbol like this one."

config HCP_MODBUS_DEFAULT_BAUD
	int "Default modbus baud"
	default 9600

config HCP_MODBUS_TIMEOUT_MS
	int "Milliseconds to wait for a slave's reply"
	default 500
	help
	  Passed to Zephyr's subsys as rx_timeout (in microseconds). Long enough for a
	  meter to answer at 9600 baud, short enough that one absent slave costs half a
	  second and not more - and after three such costs in a row the collector leaves
	  that unit alone for a cooldown (see hcp::collector::BusHealth).

config HCP_MODBUS_DEMO
	bool "Run a periodic Modbus poll demo in main"
	default n
	help
	  Bring-up aid: periodically reads holding registers from a configured
	  slave so the uart1/RS485 path can be exercised on real hardware. With no
	  slave on the bus it logs a -ENOTCONN per attempt, which still proves the
	  interface resolved, the UART is configured and DE toggles.

	  NOT implied by HCP_BENCH, and that is a measured correction rather than a
	  preference: hcp::collector also opens uart1, and with the demo on the bench
	  image both were running as masters on one half-duplex bus - the demo's
	  "poll failed (err=-116)" every 2 s buried the collector's own poll attempts
	  and made the bus unusable to observe. The question this demo used to answer
	  ("does the RTU path work?") is now answered better by publishing a point table
	  on <sn>/config over MQTT and watching the collector.

	  Do not combine it with hcp::collector, nor with the raw loopback self-test:
	  all three want the same pins.

config HCP_MODBUS_DEMO_SLAVE
	int "Demo: Modbus slave/unit address"
	default 1

endif
